/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  unsteady03_nf_feed_and_bleed_startup -- the START-UP of the feed-and-bleed
  loop whose steady state is membrane18_nf_feed_and_bleed.

  THE PLANT.  Fresh feed (1 wt % glucose, 200 kmol/h) joins the recycle in
  MIX and fills a 1 m3 recirculation tank; the tank feeds an NF270 module
  (15 m2); the retentate is split, 95 % back to MIX and 5 % bled off.  The
  permeate and the bleed leave.  At t = 0 the tank holds feed.

  WHAT IS SOLVED HOW.  The TANK is a vessel (dynamicCSTR, no reaction, no
  jacket): it holds an inventory and is integrated in time.  MIX, NF and
  SPLIT hold essentially no liquid, so on the loop's time scale they are
  at steady state at every instant -- each is its steady unit wrapped as
  `quasiSteady` and re-solved on every accepted state.  The module the
  run solves is exactly the one membrane18 solves; only the time around it
  is new.

  WHAT TO WATCH.  The tank starts at the feed concentration, so the
  module first sees the feed and delivers its single-pass flux.  As the
  retentate returns, the loop concentrates, the osmotic pressure rises
  and the flux falls towards the value membrane18 reports at steady
  state.  The time scale is the tank's contents over the flows that carry
  solute OUT of the loop -- the bleed, and the permeate because NF270
  passes glucose partly: V / (Q_bleed + Q_permeate (1 - R)) is about
  1 m3 / 5.3e-4 m3/s = 1900 s, and the loop is faster than that (measured
  e-folding about 1340 s, from successive gaps in balanceTrajectory.csv)
  because the bleed leaves at the RETENTATE concentration, richer than
  the tank's.  6 h is some 16 of them, so the end state must be
  membrane18's: the golden holds the module's flux, rejection and the
  permeate and bleed flows to membrane18's own within 2e-5.  That
  agreement is the case's anchor -- a transient that does not end on the
  steady answer is wrong.

  WHAT IS ASSUMED, and printed on the run: the quasi-steady units hold no
  inventory (nothing accumulates in the module or the pipes), the tank
  is well mixed, and the loop's pressure is the module's feed pressure
  throughout (no pump is modelled, as in membrane18 -- see its header).
  The first law is NOT claimed: the dynamic ledger needs a stored energy
  functional per unit, which the quasi-steady adapter does not provide,
  and the run says so rather than closing it with a zero.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        MIX;
        type        quasiSteady;
        inputs      ( Feed  Recycle );
        outputs     ( ToTank );
        unit
        {
            type        mixer;
            operation   { T 298.15 K; }
        }
    }
    {
        name        TANK;
        type        dynamicCSTR;
        in          ToTank;
        outputs     ( ModuleIn );
        operation
        {
            UA           0.0;             // W/K: no jacket
            T_jacket     298.15 K;
        }
    }
    {
        name        NF;
        type        quasiSteady;
        in          ModuleIn;
        outputs     ( Retentate  Permeate );    // 0 = retentate, 1 = permeate
        unit
        {
            type        spiralWoundModule;
            operation
            {
                membrane         NF270;
                area             15.0;
                length           1.0;
                P_permeate       1.0 bar;
                k_film           5.0e-5;
                dP_feed_total    0.0 bar;
                nNodes           100;
            }
        }
    }
    {
        name        SPLIT;
        type        quasiSteady;
        in          Retentate;
        outputs     ( Recycle  Bleed );
        unit
        {
            type        splitter;
            operation   { fractions ( 0.95  0.05 ); }
        }
    }
);
